+# Text performance and rendering quality — 0.3.0-alpha.1
+
+## Method
+
+The untouched, hash-verified 0.2.0-alpha.1 baseline and the continuation run the
+same benchmark script in separate Node processes. Each workload warms up, then
+records five samples. A fixed-width counting metrics provider isolates JavaScript
+layout/binding algorithms. This deliberately does not benchmark native shaping,
+browser presentation, WebGPU, CPU/GPU contention or end-user frame rate.
+
+Actual raw samples and operation counts are in `artifacts/benchmark-baseline.json`,
+`artifacts/benchmark-current.json` and `artifacts/performance-comparison.json`.
+Timing is environment-specific; the removed work is the more useful invariant.
+
+| Workload | Baseline median | Continuation median |
+| --- | ---: | ---: |
+| 5000 unchanged TextBlock renders | 14.412 ms | 2.509 ms |
+| 100 repeated caret hit tests on a 5000-character line | 200.968 ms | 0.198 ms |
+| 20 wrapped layouts of a 10000-character line | 82.057 ms | 5.195 ms |
+| 5000 nested compiled-binding leaf updates | 15.115 ms | 8.564 ms |
+
+## Removed repeated work
+
+For 5,000 unchanged TextBlock renders, layout constructions and measurements drop
+from 5,000 each to zero after warm-up. For 100 repeated hit tests on a 5,000-character
+line, prefix measurements drop from 500,100 to zero after warm-up; the first hit
+uses 12 rather than 5,001 measurements. Formatting 20 wrapped 10,000-character
+lines uses 800 rather than 200,020 measurements. Across 5,000 nested binding leaf
+updates, ancestor reads drop from 5,000 to zero and new subscriptions from 10,000
+to zero. Retaining only the affected binding suffix also avoids subscription churn
+and stale branch observers.
+
+The browser-native test independently renders the same TextBlock 1,000 times
+through actual Skia after warm-up. It verifies zero newly constructed layouts,
+zero new text measurements, zero rasterizations and zero uploads. It does not
+assert zero total drawing, allocation or GPU work.
+
+## Pixel quality
+
+Seventeen native-Skia pixel comparisons cover fractional origins and scales
+1, 1.25, 1.5, 2 and 3; italic overhang; combining marks/mixed scripts; letter spacing;
+nonuniform 1.25/1.75 scaling; and long lines spanning 128px raster tiles. The
+reference is the same browser's direct Canvas2D rendering with the same logical
+text geometry and transform. This isolates the upload/composition transport; it
+is not an independent shaping oracle or a .NET Avalonia differential test.
+
+The declared tolerance is two channel levels (8-bit). The executed comparisons
+had a maximum channel difference of 1 and no missing-ink pixels. Two additional
+checks verify viewport culling and the warm redraw behavior described above.
+For a 10,000-character line in a 400px viewport, only four visible tiles were
+rasterized/uploaded. `artifacts/screenshots/text-quality.png` shows actual Skia
+output; the machine-readable pixel data summaries are the evidence.
+
+## Limits and ownership
+
+Ordinary system-font text uses Canvas2D shaping/rasterization plus native Skia
+composition. Native SkParagraph is selected for explicitly registered faces.
+The two paths are not claimed equivalent for all scripts/fonts. Positive
+axis-aligned transforms are pixel-qualified; rotation/shear select the maximum
+affine stretch conservatively but are not covered by these pixel comparisons.
+Reflections, perspective, LCD subpixel modes and all hinting behavior remain open.
+
+Entry/estimated-byte budgets bound cache structures, not total browser/WASM heap
+usage. Typography strings may remain in bounded caches until eviction or clearing;
+these are not secure-memory stores, and no RAM zeroization guarantee is made.
+Long input still has shaping, key and iteration cost. Binary prefix searches
+assume monotonic advance widths; negative spacing retains the linear fallback.
+Rich runs and mutable feature collections avoid unsafe cache reuse.
